# Merging is a fold, so three failures answer three reasons however they were
# grouped. The merge conses a pair at each step unless it knows which errs
# were born merged, and then the same three reasons print three ways depending
# on where the parentheses fell.
#
# The wall is the other half: it is ordered, so the first failure is the answer
# and what follows never speaks. Both rules are here because the sweep that
# gave every operator the merge gave it to `>>` too, on one engine only, and
# nothing said so.
#
# The reasons are read through `std/testing`'s `when_failed`: foreign to this
# module, so it is licensed to separate a reason from a failure this module
# raised. Under gavel 24 an arm here could not see these errs at all.
#
# Since the 2026-09-15 ruling a raised err handed straight to an operator or
# to a group with no arm for it does not compile, so the failures here are
# bound to names first. The checker reads a call's arguments and not the names
# it is handed, the same deliberate blind spot the none rule keeps, and the
# runtime contract these lines pin is reached through it. What the runtime
# does with a bare err it was not warned about is the ledger's open question.
import "std/testing"

fn boom what
  err what

fn as_text reason
  "{reason}"

# A reason that is genuinely a list is one reason, and merging must not open it.
fn listed _
  err ["a" "b"]

a = boom "a"

b = boom "b"

c = boom "c"

ab = listed 0

left = a + b + c

right = a + (b + c)

nested = ab + c

walled = a .> (_ -> b)

pub play = print "left first: {testing/when_failed left as_text}"
  .> (_ -> print "right first: {testing/when_failed right as_text}")
  .> (_ -> print "list reason: {testing/when_failed nested as_text}")
  .> (_ -> print "the wall: {testing/when_failed walled as_text}")
